      <div class="case-content">
        <section id="problem">
          <h2>1. Why naive mouse-angle and polling-rate estimators fail</h2>
          <p>
            Estimating natural mouse grip rotation and movement cadence from Windows <code>WM_INPUT</code> streams exposes four numerical and physical biases routinely overlooked by ad-hoc calibrators:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Temporal packet-density bias:</strong> Windows delivers movement events in uneven bursts; slowing down near the end of a swipe clusters samples and skews standard unweighted OLS regression.</li>
            <li><strong>Wrist-arc vs. sensor-slant conflation:</strong> human wrist pivoting generates a parabolic arc <code>v(u) = a·u² + b·u</code>; adopting the parabola's origin tangent <code>b</code> as whole-stroke rotation systematically over-corrects curved strokes.</li>
            <li><strong>Catastrophic cancellation &amp; inflated <code>R²</code>:</strong> accumulating raw quartic sums <code>Σu⁴</code> over thousands of counts loses precision, and dividing residual error by <code>Σu²</code> conflates orthogonal axes and reports false confidence.</li>
            <li><strong>Jensen's inequality bias on polling rates:</strong> averaging instantaneous frequencies <code>E[1/Δt]</code> over jittery intervals strictly overestimates true rate because <code>f(x) = 1/x</code> is convex for <code>x &gt; 0</code>.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="so2">
          <h2>2. Exact SO(2) isometry &amp; uniform arc-length resampling</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Exact octant trigonometric basis (<code>Octant::basis</code> in <code>src/math.rs</code>):</strong> projects displacements onto 8 canonical sectors via an exact lookup table <code>(1, 0)</code>, <code>(1/√2, 1/√2)</code>, <code>(0, 1)</code>, etc., paired with hardware FMA (<code>mul_add</code>). This eliminates floating-point axis bleed on cardinal swipes (verified by <code>test_so2_isometry_and_zero_bleed</code>).</li>
            <li><strong>Uniform spatial resampling (<code>resample_by_distance(points, 64)</code>):</strong> integrates cumulative arc length via <code>dx.hypot(dy)</code> and resamples every trace onto <strong>64 evenly spaced points</strong> before fitting, preventing USB/<code>WM_INPUT</code> burst clustering and variable hand velocity from biasing slope or <code>R²</code>.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="regression">
          <h2>3. Numerical preconditioning &amp; parabolic vs. linear separation</h2>
          <p>
            <code>fit_projected_parabolic_arc</code> and <code>projected_linearity</code> decouple curvature diagnostics from global slant over the same resampled trace:
          </p>
          <div class="paper-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="en-p17-models">
            <table class="paper-table">
              <caption id="en-p17-models">Table 1 · Preconditioned models in <code>src/math.rs</code> (characteristic scale <code>L = max(|u|)</code>, normalized coordinates in <code>[-1, 1]</code>)</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Model</th>
                  <th scope="col">Formulation &amp; conditioning</th>
                  <th scope="col">Calibration role</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Projected parabolic arc</th>
                  <td data-label="Formulation"><code>v(u) = a·u² + b·u</code> with <code>ũ = u/L</code>, determinant <code>D = s₄s₂ − s₃²</code> guarded by <code>D &gt; 10⁻¹¹·s₄s₂</code>, and Horner + FMA residual evaluation.</td>
                  <td data-label="Role">Quantifies biomechanical wrist curvature (<code>wrist_curvature = a</code>) independent of stroke length.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Origin-constrained line</th>
                  <td data-label="Formulation"><code>v(u) = b·u</code> with <code>b = Σ(ũṽ)/Σ(ũ²)</code>, angle <code>θ = atan(b)</code>, and Welford online cross-track total variance <code>ss_tot</code>.</td>
                  <td data-label="Role">Computes whole-stroke slant (<code>slant_deg</code>) and <code>linearity_r_squared</code> (preserving negative <code>R²</code> when worse than mean-only baseline) to accept or reject traces.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="huber">
          <h2>4. Robust aggregation via IRLS Huber M-estimator &amp; 12-swipe convergence gate</h2>
          <p>
            A single accidental wrist twitch can pull an arithmetic mean off target. <code>RobustCalibrator</code> (<code>src/controller.rs</code>) enforces two-stage robust estimation:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Per-swipe qualification gate (<code>is_swipe_qualified</code>):</strong> requires finite values, <code>path_length ≥ 450</code> counts, <code>linearity_r_squared ≥ 0.70</code>, and <code>|θ| ≤ 32.0°</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>IRLS Huber location estimator (<code>compute_huber_location</code>):</strong> initializes <code>μ₀</code> at the sample median over a 12-swipe rolling window, sets adaptive cutoff <code>δ = max(1.345 · σ_θ, 0.5)</code>, and runs 5 IRLS iterations with weight <code>wᵢ = 1</code> when <code>|xᵢ − μ| ≤ δ</code> else <code>δ / |xᵢ − μ|</code>. On contaminated input <code>[3.0, 3.1, 3.2, 3.3, 3.4, 25.0]</code>, the sample mean shifts to <code>6.83°</code> while Huber remains near <code>3.3°</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Strict convergence lock (<code>auto_lock_ready</code>):</strong> locks the recommended angle only when <code>n ≥ 12</code> qualified swipes, <code>mean_R² ≥ 0.94</code>, and sample spread <code>σ_θ ≤ 1.15°</code> hold simultaneously.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="jensen">
          <h2>5. Jensen's inequality immunity in polling telemetry &amp; CRC-32 interlock</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Zero-allocation nanosecond ring buffer (<code>PollingAnalyzer&lt;const N: usize = 64&gt;</code> in <code>src/polling_radar.rs</code>):</strong> stores 64 nanosecond inter-arrival deltas on the stack, sums in <code>u128</code> without floating-point accumulation drift, and reports harmonic mean rate <code>f̄ = 10⁹ / E[Δt_ns]</code> rather than <code>E[10⁹ / Δt_ns]</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Exact Jensen immunity verification (<code>test_formula5_jensen_inequality_immunity</code>):</strong> given 64 alternating <code>1.2 ms</code> / <code>0.8 ms</code> intervals (true mean <code>1.0 ms</code>, population jitter <code>0.2 ms</code>), naive frequency averaging falsely reports <code>(833.33 + 1250)/2 = 1041.67 Hz</code> (+4.17% inflation); <code>PollingAnalyzer&lt;64&gt;</code> recovers exact <strong><code>1000.0 Hz</code></strong> (error <code>&lt; 10⁻⁹</code>), <code>mean = 1.0 ms</code>, and <code>jitter = 0.2 ms</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Keyboard counter-strafe &amp; sensitivity parity:</strong> <code>keyboard.rs</code> tracks A/D overlap and release gap timing under a <strong>CRC-32</strong>-verified tournament-safe interlock, while <code>aim_trainer.rs</code> preserves exact <code>cm/360°</code> parity between CS2 (<code>yaw = 0.022</code>) and Valorant (<code>yaw = 0.07</code>, exact ratio <code>35/11</code>).</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="validity">
